.TH FKTOUPPER 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fktoupper, fktolower \- convert the case of an fkstring in place
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fktoupper(fkstring *" fks );
.BI "fkstring *fktolower(fkstring *" fks );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fktoupper ()
converts the ASCII letters
.BR a \(en z
in
.I fks
to uppercase, and
.BR fktolower ()
converts
.BR A \(en Z
to lowercase, in place.
All
.I len
bytes are converted, including any after an embedded NUL byte.
.PP
The conversion is byte-wise and ASCII-only.
It does not depend on the locale, unlike
.BR toupper (3)
and
.BR tolower (3),
and it leaves every byte outside
.BR A \(en Z
and
.BR a \(en z
unchanged.
In particular, it is not UTF-8 aware: multibyte characters such as
.B \(aq\('e\(aq
are not converted.
.BR fktolower ()
folds case exactly as
.BR fkstrcasecmp (3)
does, so two strings compare equal under
.BR fkstrcasecmp ()
if and only if their lowercased copies are equal under
.BR fkstreq (3).
.PP
The buffer is never reallocated, and an empty
.I fks
is left unchanged.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.IR fks ,
for chaining, or NULL if
.I fks
is NULL.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s = fkstrnew("Hello, World!");
\&
fktoupper(s);    /* s is "HELLO, WORLD!" */
fktolower(s);    /* s is "hello, world!" */
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkstrcmp (3),
.BR toupper (3)
